  "title": "Erdős Problem #960",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $r,k\\geq 2$ be fixed. Let $A\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a set of $n$ points with no $k$ points on a line. Determine the threshold $f_{r,k}(n)$ such that if there are at least $f_{r,k}(n)$ many ordinary lines (lines containing exactly two points) then there is a set $A'\\subseteq A$ of $r$ points such that all $\\binom{r}{2}$ many lines determined by $A'$ are ordinary.\nIs it true that $f_{r,k}(n)=o(n^2)$, or perhaps even $\\ll n$?",
    "sha256": "152227d1c77dd632a2dbb15e4d2d68e064cc94be2b7e2509c15e2c5568040031",
